WEBVTT

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.200
AI is drinking India's water.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.720
So here's the question nobody was asking: can AI even see where that water goes?

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:27.620
India's data centres drink about 150 billion litres of water a year. By 2030? 358 billion.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:34.465
And seventy percent of that capacity sits in two cities already short of water: Mumbai and Chennai.

00:00:34.585 --> 00:00:43.985
Now we're building AI agents to watch over it, from maps and satellite images. And it all comes down to one question: where does the wastewater go?

00:00:45.085 --> 00:00:47.135
So we built a factory.

00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:56.755
Code raises a landscape, makes it rain, and follows every drop downhill. Past a data centre. Past villages. Past traps we hid on purpose.

00:00:56.875 --> 00:01:00.925
Which means it knows the answer before it ever asks the question.

00:01:01.045 --> 00:01:05.645
Then it paints the same scene three ways: satellite, map, and thermal.

00:01:06.745 --> 00:01:10.270
Ten reasoning skills. Not one of them is counting.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:20.265
Five levels of difficulty. Real terrain from four Indian data-centre cities. Monsoon, haze, a blurry sensor. And the answer never moves.

00:01:20.385 --> 00:01:25.860
Five thousand seven hundred and sixty-one tasks. Every one with a known answer.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.735
Then we did something sneaky. Every scene gets a twin.

00:01:30.855 --> 00:01:36.455
Change one small thing, move the outfall to another stream, and the right answer flips.

00:01:36.575 --> 00:01:41.525
A model that's really looking gets both. A model running on habit? Caught.

00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:46.400
Now, you shouldn't have to trust us. So we don't ask you to.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:55.620
A completely separate hydrology library re-derives every answer. If it disagrees, the question is gone. Dropped. Never edited.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.415
The answer has to be visible in the actual pixels, or it doesn't ship.

00:02:00.535 --> 00:02:07.860
We even turned the audit on ourselves: four hundred and thirty-one unfair tasks. Found, excluded, published.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.305
And every audited image rebuilds from its seed, byte for byte.

00:02:13.405 --> 00:02:15.655
And you don't need us to run it.

00:02:15.775 --> 00:02:21.800
One pip install. One command. It builds a scene and its twin, and runs every check.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.345
Every check you see passing here really passed. If it hadn't, the question would be gone.

00:02:28.445 --> 00:02:33.495
So we put the models to the test. The same eighty-three questions, for everyone.

00:02:33.615 --> 00:02:38.740
The open models? No better than guessing, and guessing gets you fourteen percent.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:47.260
Gemini Flash-Lite gets forty to forty-eight percent. Hide the image, and it falls straight back to guessing. It's really reading the map.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:52.630
And Gemini 3.5 Flash? Seventy-seven percent. Impressive.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.605
But then, the twins.

00:02:55.725 --> 00:03:01.025
Same river. We just cooled the water, so no point is above three degrees any more.

00:03:01.145 --> 00:03:05.620
Gemini gives the exact same answer. Two false heat alarms.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:12.015
Across nineteen pairs, it got both halves right only six times. Every other model, two at most.

00:03:13.115 --> 00:03:18.840
But a right answer isn't the whole story. So we asked the models to draw the water's path.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:27.460
Eight times, a model followed the water, then named the wrong villages. Seven times, it named the right villages along the wrong route.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:32.830
Then we hid part of the river under a cloud, where the answer truly cannot be known.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:40.475
Thirty-four times out of forty-two, the models made up an answer anyway. Only two models always said: I can't tell.

00:03:41.575 --> 00:03:47.425
Every real reply is scored live. Right or wrong. And we tell you which trap it fell into.

00:03:47.545 --> 00:03:51.595
Flip on the answer key, and watch where the water really goes.

00:03:51.715 --> 00:03:54.965
Or skip the models, and take the test yourself.

00:03:56.065 --> 00:04:00.465
And this isn't a one-off study. It's a benchmark anyone can use.

00:04:00.585 --> 00:04:06.710
Every model gets a report card: skills, pairs, traps, and what happens without the image.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:14.780
Got your own model? Download the pack, run it, drop the replies on the page. Same report card. Scored right in your browser.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:27.155
And here's the hopeful part. Train a small model on these scenes, and on scenes it has never seen, it climbs from ten to nineteen percent. Only with the image. It learns to look.

00:04:27.275 --> 00:04:30.900
And people? They can take the very same test, online.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:38.050
There's a Colab notebook, a pip-installable command, and automatic checks on every change.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:45.120
And the frontier test runs on a free tier: one request per image, about three hundred tokens an answer.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:53.370
ThirstMap. A factory of visual tasks with verified answers, and a test AI can't bluff its way through.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:58.265
Because before we trust AI with India's water, it should have to pass it.
